In this way, the determination of meditation subjects (kammaṭṭhāna) should first be known by these ten aspects.

[107] Among these ten aspects, (1) "By way of detailed explanation of the meditation subjects summarized by the number forty":

Note: It could also be translated as—"Because I said it will become clear by itself in the detailed explanation of the table of contents (mātika)"—this causal statement (hetu) should be taken in connection with the words "the determination of meditation subjects should be known by these ten aspects." Among those ten aspects, the heading "classification of suitability to temperament" is most relevant to that reason.

In the statement "among the forty meditation subjects," these are the forty meditation subjects: ten kasinas, ten kinds of foulness (asubha), ten recollections (anussati), four divine abidings (brahmavihāra), four immaterial states (āruppa), one perception (saññā), and one defining (vavatthāna).

Among these forty meditation subjects, the ten kasinas are: earth kasina, water kasina, fire kasina, air kasina, blue kasina, yellow kasina, red kasina, white kasina, light kasina, and limited-space kasina.

The ten kinds of foulness are: the bloated, the livid, the festering, the cut-up, the gnawed, the scattered, the hacked and scattered, the bleeding, the worm-infested, and the skeleton.

The ten recollections are: recollection of the Buddha, recollection of the Dhamma, recollection of the Saṅgha, recollection of virtue, recollection of generosity, recollection of deities, recollection of death, mindfulness occupied with the body, mindfulness of breathing, and recollection of peace.

The four divine abidings are: loving-kindness, compassion, appreciative joy, and equanimity.

The four immaterial states are: the base of infinite space, the base of infinite consciousness, the base of nothingness, and the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception.

Note 1: The meanings of these Pali names for meditation subjects will become clear in the detailed explanation. Therefore, in this section, they are only briefly presented for ease of reference without defining their meanings.

The perception of repulsiveness in nutriment is the one perception. The defining of the four elements is the one defining. Thus, the determination should be known by way of detailed explanation of the meditation subjects summarized by the number forty.

(2) "By way of bringing about access and absorption":
Excluding mindfulness occupied with the body and mindfulness of breathing, the remaining eight recollections, the one perception of repulsiveness in nutriment, and the one defining of the four elements—these ten meditation subjects bring about only access [Note 1: Meaning they can cause or obtain access concentration (upacāra-samādhi)]. The remaining thirty meditation subjects (from those forty) bring about absorption [Note 2: Meaning they can cause or obtain up to absorption jhana (appanā-jhāna)]. Thus, the determination should be known by way of bringing about access and absorption.

(3) "By way of classification of jhana":
Among those thirty meditation subjects that bring about absorption, the ten kasinas together with mindfulness of breathing are the objects of the four jhanas. The ten kinds of foulness together with mindfulness occupied with the body are the objects of the first jhana. The first three divine abidings (loving-kindness, compassion, appreciative joy) are the objects of the [lower] three jhanas. The fourth divine abiding and the four immaterial states are the objects of the fourth jhana. Thus, the determination should be known by way of classification of jhana.

(4) "By way of transcending factors and objects":
There are two kinds of transcending: transcending of factors (aṅga-samatikkama) and transcending of objects (ārammaṇa-samatikkama).
